A picture for the displays to start with, until the application has drawn

Cage gives a display an empty frame when it takes it, and the display is
black from then until the application has put something on it - which for
an application that takes a while to start, on a machine that had a
picture on its screen from the boot loader, is a black gap between two
pictures.

With CAGE_SPLASH in the environment naming a PNG file, a display is given
that picture instead: it is in the first frame Cage gives the display, and
stays until the application has drawn. The picture is in the middle of
the display and the rest of the display is the colour of the picture's
first pixel. It is shown at the largest whole fraction of its size - all
of it, a half, a third - that is no wider than half the display and no
taller than the display, each pixel the mean of the pixels it stands for,
so that a picture drawn large is sharp on a display of any size. Only that
mean is rounded: a boot loader that does the same sums has shown the same
picture the same to the pixel (GRUB does, with the patch that gives its
background_image a splash mode), and nothing is seen to change when Cage
takes the display from it. What alpha the picture has is not looked at.

When the application has drawn is for the application to say, since its
window is mapped before it has anything to show, and a window that is
partly clear by design - the OpenGVA HMI's, which shows other programs'
windows through itself - is clear all over until then. It has drawn when
a window says that some of itself is opaque: with -u a window of the
application Cage was started for, and without it any window. Its word is
checked, though. A toolkit sends what it has been told is opaque with the
next thing it draws, which may be a frame before the one that is true of,
and the window is still clear then: the splash taken away at that would
leave the display black until the frame came. So a pixel of what the
window calls opaque is looked at, and the splash stays until that pixel
is. Then it is taken away and not shown again, and what is behind the
windows is what it always was.

The look is taken after a frame has been given to a display, and not when
the window commits. With a GPU, looking at a pixel that is still being
drawn waits for the drawing, and the frame the pixel is in is not to wait
for that: it goes to the display with the splash still under its windows,
and the splash is gone from the next.

It is an environment variable and not an option so that a script written
for this Cage still starts one without the patch.

The picture is read with libpng, which Cage now needs.

Upstream: not submitted

